            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to hinder, then the Latin for to throw into confusion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: contingō, contingere",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "contingō, contingere — \"to touch; to happen (to), to fall to one's lot\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mihi contigit ut eum vidērem. I had the good luck to see him."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "contingō, contingere, contigī, contactum. Built on tangō, to touch. Contingit + dative, often of good luck. English contact and contingent."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "contingō is built from con- and tangō \"to touch\" (EtymDB; Lewis & Short), the vowel weakened inside the compound: what touches you is what happens to you. English contact and contingent come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
